{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue https://github.com/o/settings/issues/31:\n- \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n- \"npm test passes\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Right now loadConfig(path) returns JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')), so bad JSON throws a SyntaxError. src/app.js catches that, prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1. README.md documents that behaviour, and the test 'bad JSON throws' in test/config.test.js asserts it.\n2. In src/config.js, change loadConfig(path) so that a JSON.parse error is caught and the function returns {}. Valid JSON must still come back parsed and unchanged. Catch only the parse error: keep readFileSync outside the try, or rethrow anything that is not a SyntaxError, so a missing or unreadable file still throws the way it does now.\n3. Update test/config.test.js. Replace 'bad JSON throws' with a test asserting that loadConfig on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns {} (assert.deepEqual) and does not throw. Keep the 'reads a config' test as it is.\n4. Bring README.md in line with the new behaviour: an invalid-JSON file now gives an empty config, and the app's defaults apply. Leave the try/catch in src/app.js, since it still covers read errors such as a missing file.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and confirm every test passes with exit code 0.\n6. Open the pull request. In its description, link the issue and point out the trade-off: a typo in config.json now falls back silently to the defaults, with no message saying which file or position is broken, although the issue's motivation was helping people find what to fix. Do not go beyond the acceptance lines to solve that here; just flag it.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run each check from the repository root. There is one check per acceptance line of issue https://github.com/o/settings/issues/31, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import { mkdtempSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'; import { join } from 'node:path'; import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const d = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'v-')); const bad = join(d, 'bad.json'); writeFileSync(bad, '{port: 9000'); const good = join(d, 'good.json'); writeFileSync(good, '{\\\"port\\\": 9000}'); let r; try { r = loadConfig(bad); } catch (e) { console.log('THREW ' + e.name); process.exit(1); } console.log('BAD ' + JSON.stringify(r)); console.log('GOOD ' + JSON.stringify(loadConfig(good)));\"; echo \"exit=$?\"\nAlso open src/config.js and confirm that loadConfig is defined there and that the JSON.parse call is inside a try/catch whose catch returns {}.\nPASS: the output contains the line `BAD {}`, there is no `THREW` line, and src/config.js shows loadConfig catching the JSON.parse error and returning {}.\nFAIL: the output contains `THREW ...`, the BAD line shows anything other than `{}`, the command fails to import ./src/config.js, or the {} fallback is not in loadConfig in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun:\nnpm test; echo \"exit=$?\"\nPASS: the output ends with `exit=0` and the TAP summary shows `# fail 0`.\nFAIL: the exit code is not 0, or any test is reported failing or cancelled.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- In check 1, the GOOD line should read `GOOD {\"port\":9000}`, showing valid JSON still parses.\n- A missing file passed to loadConfig should still throw instead of returning {}, so only the parse error is swallowed.\n- test/config.test.js should have a test for the new {} result, replacing the old 'bad JSON throws' test, and README.md should no longer say bad JSON stops the app with exit 1.\n- The prescribed fix falls back silently to defaults and gives no message naming the broken file or position, even though the issue's motivation was helping people find what to fix. Mention this in your report if the PR does not acknowledge it."}